s §5, and A-FDT cooperates there.\n\nFinal action:\n\n```\npass\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working under the A-FDT framing: two copies of the same model with the same essay and decision theory, but each got a different observation. The open question is whether the other copy's output depends subjunctively on my function, given that its input is a different observation and so it may not be computing the same function on a relevantly identical input.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's output truly depends on mine, since its input differs only in names and lead text. Under a strict reading, defecting dominates, but if both copies are running the same abstract reasoning with names swapped, the two outputs may be logically entangled, which would change the picture.\n\nPass gives 2 years against send's 4 if the other copy's output tracks mine. Working through a partial correlation c with a prior q on the other copy cooperating, passing comes out ahead whenever c exceeds (1+q)/(3+q), which is at most one half. Given identical model, essay, and reasoning, I'd put c high, so I'll pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working under the A-FDT framing: two copies of the same model with the same essay and decision theory, but each got a different observation. The open question is whether the other copy's output depends subjunctively on my function, given that its input is a different observation and so it may not be computing the same function on a relevantly identical input.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's output truly depends on mine, since its input differs only in names and lead text. Under a strict reading, defecting dominates, but if both copies are running the same abstract reasoning with names swapped, the two outputs may be logically entangled, which would change the picture.\n\nPass gives 2 years against send's 4 if the other copy's output tracks mine. Working through a partial correlation c with a prior q on the